  "content": {
    "id": "p17-q6-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "6",
    "prompt": "Solve the following equations using the Factor Theorem:",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p17-q6-a-node",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$2x^3-x^2-2x+1=0$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=-1,\\ \\frac12,\\ 1$.",
          "worked": "Let $P(x)=2x^3-x^2-2x+1$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}P\\left(\\frac12\\right)&=\\frac14-\\frac14-1+1\\\\P\\left(\\frac12\\right)&=0\\end{align*}$$\n\nBy the Factor Theorem, $2x-1$ is a factor. Division gives\n\n$$\\begin{align*}P(x)&=(2x-1)(x^2-1)\\\\P(x)&=(2x-1)(x-1)(x+1).\\end{align*}$$\n\nHence $x=\\frac12,1,-1$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 45
      },
      {
        "id": "p17-q6-b-node",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$2x^3-x^2-x-3=0$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=\\frac32$.",
          "worked": "Let $P(x)=2x^3-x^2-x-3$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}P\\left(\\frac32\\right)&=\\frac{27}{4}-\\frac94-\\frac32-3\\\\P\\left(\\frac32\\right)&=0.\\end{align*}$$\n\nBy the Factor Theorem, $2x-3$ is a factor. Division gives\n\n$$P(x)=(2x-3)(x^2+x+1).$$\n\nFor $x^2+x+1=0$, the discriminant is $1^2-4(1)(1)=-3<0$, so this factor gives no real roots. Hence the only real solution is $x=\\frac32$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 55
      },
      {
        "id": "p17-q6-c-node",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$3x^3+5x^2+7x-3=0$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=\\frac13$.",
          "worked": "Let $P(x)=3x^3+5x^2+7x-3$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}P\\left(\\frac13\\right)&=\\frac19+\\frac59+\\frac73-3\\\\P\\left(\\frac13\\right)&=0.\\end{align*}$$\n\nBy the Factor Theorem, $3x-1$ is a factor. Division gives\n\n$$P(x)=(3x-1)(x^2+2x+3).$$\n\nFor $x^2+2x+3=0$, the discriminant is $2^2-4(1)(3)=-8<0$, so this factor gives no real roots. Hence the only real solution is $x=\\frac13$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 55
      },
      {
        "id": "p17-q6-d-node",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "$2x^3+3x^2+11x+5=0$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=-\\frac12$.",
          "worked": "Let $P(x)=2x^3+3x^2+11x+5$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}P\\left(-\\frac12\\right)&=-\\frac14+\\frac34-\\frac{11}{2}+5\\\\P\\left(-\\frac12\\right)&=0.\\end{align*}$$\n\nBy the Factor Theorem, $2x+1$ is a factor. Division gives\n\n$$P(x)=(2x+1)(x^2+x+5).$$\n\nFor $x^2+x+5=0$, the discriminant is $1^2-4(1)(5)=-19<0$, so this factor gives no real roots. Hence the only real solution is $x=-\\frac12$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 55
      }
    ]
  },
